analysis:
The PR changes one documentation table row in docs/architecture/prefix-cache.md, at base and head line 689. The gpt-oss-20b native-checkpoint layout sizes change from float32 K/V on 23 of 24 layers (6.03 MB window, 50.33 MB per 1,024 tokens, 308.0 MB at 6,144 tokens) to bf16 K/V on all 24 layers (3.15 MB, 25.17 MB, 154.1 MB). The new figures are internally consistent: 24,576 B per token is half of 49,152.

The change is descriptive sizing only. It does not alter:
- the cache gates, TTL or disk budget;
- HMAC naming or DBK3 metadata/AAD;
- cacheSalt scoping or purge points;
- staging reservation release paths.

Those are the T-041 review triggers. Within this unit the at-rest exposure in T-041 and SEC-035 is unchanged in kind. Smaller files reduce disk and staging pressure (T-016 and availability) rather than increasing it.

One point needs confirming outside this batch. If the underlying code actually changed the gpt-oss KV dtype, the observed-dtype storage identity should still bind the dtype into the fingerprint and namespace. That binding is described at lines 427-432 (CompleteCheckpointStorageIdentity.swift, PAGED_KV_DTYPE probe at lines 98-102), so old float32 files would fail cold rather than be misinterpreted. No code is in this batch, so that binding is not verified here. Within the supplied evidence no security regression or threat-model update need was identified.
